          Executive Summary: Advantage vs. Supplement
        </div>
        <ul class="callout-list">
          <li><strong>The Core Trade-off:</strong> Medicare Advantage keeps monthly bills low (<strong>often $0 beyond Part B</strong>), but locks you into local doctor networks and prior-authorization rules. Medigap charges a higher monthly premium (<strong>$130–$250/mo</strong>), yet lets you visit any U.S. doctor who takes Medicare with almost no copays.</li>
          <li><strong>Medigap Gold Standard:</strong> <strong>Plan G</strong> picks up 100% of your Medicare-approved costs once you pay the annual Part B deductible ($257 in 2026).</li>
          <li><strong>The One-Way Door:</strong> During your 6-month Medigap Open Enrollment Window (triggered when you are 65+ and enrolled in Part B), insurers <em>must</em> sell you a policy regardless of your health. Pick Medicare Advantage first and develop a chronic condition later, and Medigap insurers can turn you down.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p class="lead-text">
        More than 4 million Americans hit age 65 each year and run straight into a fork in the road: stick with Original Medicare and add a Medigap supplement, or hand your benefits over to a private Medicare Advantage (Part C) plan. Getting that call right can save you thousands of dollars and a lot of red tape later in retirement.
      </p>

      <h2>Side-by-Side Comparison: Medicare Advantage vs. Medigap (Plan G & Plan N)</h2>
      <p>
        Here is how both options stack up side by side under 2026 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) rules:
      </p>

