      <h2>Understanding Medicare Advantage (Medicare Part C)</h2>
      <p>
        Medicare Advantage plans are run by private insurers such as UnitedHealthcare, Humana, CVS/Aetna, and Kaiser Permanente. When you sign up, Medicare pays that company a flat monthly fee to manage your care and pay your claims.
      </p>

      <h3>The Advantages of Part C</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Low or $0 Monthly Premium:</strong> In most metro counties, you can find plans that charge $0 a month on top of your standard Medicare Part B premium.</li>
        <li><strong>One Card for Everything:</strong> Hospital care, doctor visits, prescription drugs, and small dental or eyewear allowances usually sit under one plan ID.</li>
        <li><strong>A Built-In Spending Cap:</strong> Original Medicare has no annual ceiling on what you can pay out of pocket. Advantage plans cap your yearly in-network hospital and doctor bills at $8,850 or less.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>The Serious Drawbacks & Hidden Costs of Part C</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Prior Authorization Roadblocks:</strong> Audits by the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Office of Inspector General found that Medicare Advantage insurers wrongly denied tens of thousands of care requests that Original Medicare would have approved.</li>
        <li><strong>Tight Local Networks:</strong> If you travel often or spend winters in Florida or Arizona, an HMO Advantage plan usually will not cover routine care outside your home service area.</li>
        <li><strong>Steep Pay-As-You-Go Copays:</strong> A $0 monthly bill feels great while you are healthy. A hospital stay, chemotherapy, or skilled nursing rehab changes the math fast, with inpatient copays commonly running $300 to $400 a day for the first 5 to 7 days.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Understanding Medicare Supplement Insurance (Medigap)</h2>
      <p>
        Medigap plans are standardized private policies that sit behind Original Medicare and pay what it leaves behind. That mainly means the 20% Part B coinsurance on doctor bills and the Part A inpatient hospital deductible ($1,676 in 2026).
      </p>

      <h3>Why Medigap Plan G Is the Gold Standard</h3>
      <p>
        Plan F is closed to anyone who became eligible for Medicare after January 1, 2020, which leaves <strong>Plan G</strong> at the top of the lineup. Once you pay the annual Part B deductible ($257) early in the year, Plan G pays 100% of your remaining Medicare-approved costs for the rest of the calendar year. No surprise hospital bills, no network guesswork.
      </p>

      <h3>Medigap Plan N: The Lower-Cost Alternative</h3>
      <p>
        Want the same nationwide doctor access as Plan G without the top-shelf monthly bill? <strong>Plan N</strong> typically runs 25% to 35% cheaper. In return, you share a few small costs:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Up to a $20 copay for doctor office visits;</li>
        <li>Up to a $50 copay for emergency room trips that do not result in an inpatient hospital stay;</li>
        <li>Part B excess charges, which are rare extra fees allowed in states that do not prohibit balance billing.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The Critical Medigap Guaranteed Issue Window</h2>
      <p>
        Pay close attention to this rule: your <strong>Medigap Open Enrollment Period</strong> runs for exactly <strong>six months</strong>, starting the first day of the month you are both 65 or older and enrolled in Medicare Part B. For full annual deadlines and plan-switching rules, see our <a href="/guides/medicare-open-enrollment/" style="color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600; text-decoration: underline;">Medicare Open Enrollment 2026 definitive guide</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout-warning">
        <div class="callout-title">⚠️ The Medical Underwriting Trap</div>
        <p class="callout-text">
          During those six months, insurers cannot turn you away or charge extra because of cancer, diabetes, heart disease, or rheumatoid arthritis. Miss that window and, in 46 states, carriers can pull your medical records, hike your rate, or reject your application completely.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>How to Decide: 4 Questions to Find Your Path</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Do you travel often or split time between two homes?</strong> Medigap is usually the smarter pick because any Medicare-participating hospital in all 50 states will take it.</li>
        <li><strong>Do you want access to top specialty centers?</strong> Major cancer hospitals such as Memorial Sloan Kettering and MD Anderson turn away many Medicare Advantage HMO plans, but they accept Original Medicare and Medigap.</li>
        <li><strong>How tight is your monthly cash flow?</strong> If an extra $150 to $200 a month squeezes your budget, Medicare Advantage gives you a low-premium starting point with bundled extras.</li>
        <li><strong>How much insurer pushback can you tolerate?</strong> If you do not want a private insurer second-guessing your doctor's orders for an MRI or surgery, Medigap keeps those decisions between you and your physician.</li>
      </ol>

