        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Medicare Plans</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Can I switch from Medicare Advantage back to Medigap later? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            You can leave Medicare Advantage during Fall Open Enrollment (Oct 15 – Dec 7) or Medicare Advantage Open Enrollment (Jan 1 – Mar 31) and move back to Original Medicare. Buying a Medigap plan afterward is the harder part. Unless you qualify for a federal guaranteed-issue right, or live in a state with continuous enrollment or birthday-switching rules (such as CT, NY, ME, or CA), private Medigap insurers can review your health history and decline your application.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Does Medigap cover dental, vision, and hearing? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            No. Standardized Medigap policies (Plans A through N) skip routine cleanings, dentures, glasses, and hearing aids. Most people on Medigap either buy a separate dental and vision policy, join a discount plan, or pay routine costs out of pocket.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What is the Medicare Part B premium for 2026? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            The standard Part B premium is projected around $185 a month. Higher earners with modified adjusted gross income above $106,000 (single) or $212,000 (married filing jointly) pay an added IRMAA surcharge on both Part B and Part D.
          </div>
        </div>
